      text: "When fifteen-year-old Yael Cohen agreed to be interviewed by a local radio station, she thought only a few people would listen. The journalist had heard about the app she had developed to help elderly people order medicine online, and he wanted to know more.\n\nAt the beginning of the interview, he asked Yael how she had got the idea. She explained that her grandmother had once waited for three hours at a pharmacy and had come home exhausted. Yael said that she had wanted to find a simple solution. The journalist then asked whether the app was difficult to use. Yael replied that she had tested it with ten neighbours over the age of seventy and that all of them had managed to use it after one short lesson.\n\nThe most surprising moment came at the end. The journalist asked Yael what her plans for the future were. She answered honestly that she did not know yet. She admitted that she sometimes found school boring and that she preferred building things to taking tests. \"But my teachers told me not to give up,\" she added, \"and they were right.\"\n\nThe next morning, a short clip of the interview was shared online, and within two days it had been watched more than half a million times. Several companies contacted Yael and offered to help her improve the app. When reporters asked her how she felt, she said that she was grateful but a little scared. \"My grandmother warned me not to let it go to my head,\" she laughed. \"So I'm still doing my homework.\"",
