      text: "Akko, which lies on the northern coast of Israel, is one of the oldest cities in the world that people still live in. Visitors who walk through its narrow streets are walking through four thousand years of history. Many of the buildings that we see today were built on top of older ones, so the city is often described as a city of layers.\n\nThe most famous period in Akko's history is the time of the Crusaders, who ruled the city in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. They built huge halls, tunnels and churches. For hundreds of years, these structures were buried under the Ottoman city, which was built in the eighteenth century. In the twentieth century, archaeologists began to dig, and they discovered the Knights' Halls, where the Crusader knights once ate and slept. Today these halls are among the city's most popular landmarks.\n\nAkko is also home to a secret tunnel which connected the fortress to the port. It was found by accident in 1994, when a woman whose sink was blocked called a plumber. While he was working, he discovered a passage under her house. The tunnel, which is about 150 metres long, is now open to the public.\n\nAkko is not only a museum, however. About fifty thousand people live in the city, which has Jewish, Muslim, Christian and Baha'i residents. In 2001, the Old City was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. For anyone who is interested in history, food or the sea, it is truly worth visiting.",
