      text: "In September 1928, a Scottish scientist named Alexander Fleming returned to his laboratory after a summer holiday. Before leaving, he had stacked several dishes of bacteria on a bench instead of cleaning them. When he came back, he noticed something strange: a mould had grown on one of the dishes, and the bacteria around it had died.\n\nMany scientists would have simply thrown the dish away. Fleming, however, was curious. He realized that the mould was producing a substance that killed bacteria. He called it penicillin. If he had cleaned his bench before his holiday, he might never have made the discovery. If he hadn't been so curious, the dish would have ended up in the rubbish.\n\nAt first, few people paid attention. Fleming could not produce penicillin in large quantities, and for about ten years the discovery was almost forgotten. Then, at the beginning of the Second World War, a team at Oxford University found a way to purify it. Thanks to their work, penicillin was produced on a large scale by 1944, and it saved the lives of thousands of injured soldiers.\n\nHistorians often describe penicillin as a turning point in medicine. Before antibiotics, a small cut could lead to a deadly infection. If penicillin hadn't been developed, millions of people who are alive today would not exist, because their grandparents would not have survived.\n\nIn hindsight, Fleming's story teaches an important lesson: great discoveries need more than luck. They also need someone who is willing to notice and ask \"why?\"",
